  "Number": 39,
  "Question": "The hypotenuse \\(c\\) and one arm \\(a\\) of a right triangle are consecutive integers. The square of the second arm is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(ca\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac ca\\)",
    "C": "\\(c+a\\)",
    "D": "\\(c-a\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Let \\(b\\) be the length of the second arm. Since the hypotenuse is the longest side, \\(c>a\\). The two lengths are consecutive integers, so \\(c-a=1\\).\n\nBy the Pythagorean theorem, the square we want is \\(b^2=c^2-a^2\\). Factoring exposes the known difference \\(c-a\\):\n\\[\nb^2=(c-a)(c+a)=1\\cdot(c+a)=c+a.\n\\]\nThus the square of the second arm is \\(\\boxed{c+a}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
